(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-cloture"]={"plain":{"bills":{"cloture":{"slug":"cloture","title":"Majority Rule in the House and Senate","sections":[{"anchor":"part-1","heading":"SECTION 1. Cloture on measures by majority","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a) The threshold.** Paragraph 2 of Rule XXII is amended\u2014\n\n(1) in the sentence beginning \"``Is it the sense of the Senate that the debate shall be brought to a close?''\", so that the sentence reads as follows:\n\n> ``Is it the sense of the Senate that the debate shall be brought to a close?'' And if that question shall be decided in the affirmative by ~~three-fifths of the Senators duly chosen and sworn--except on a measure or motion to amend the Senate rules, in which case the necessary affirmative vote shall be two-thirds of the Senators present and voting--~~ **a majority of the Senators voting, a quorum being present,** then said measure, motion, or other matter pending before the Senate, or the unfinished business, shall be the unfinished business to the exclusion of all other business until disposed of.\n\n(2) in the sentence beginning \"The thirty hours may be increased\", so that the sentence reads as follows:\n\n> The thirty hours may be increased by the adoption of a motion, decided without debate, by ~~a three-fifths affirmative vote of the Senators duly chosen and sworn~~ **a majority of the Senators voting, a quorum being present**, and any such time thus agreed upon shall be equally divided between and controlled by the Majority and Minority Leaders or their designees.\n\n**(b) What is displaced, and this subsection is general.** Any requirement \u2014 whether in these rules, in a standing order, or in a statute enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power of the Senate \u2014 **that a question be decided by more than a majority of the Senators voting is of no further effect in the Senate.** **The subsection applies whatever the question**: cloture, a motion to waive or to suspend a point of order, an appeal from a ruling of the Chair, a motion to proceed, or any other motion. **A requirement established only by the precedents of the Senate, including the requirement of two-thirds of the Senators present to suspend a rule, is not displaced.**\n\n**(c) Those voting.** A Senator not voting is not counted on either side. The threshold is a majority of those voting; a quorum must be present. When the Senators voting are equally divided, the Vice President may vote, and that vote decides the question.\n\n**(d) The budget process is left to the companion resolution.** Subsection (b) does not reach a requirement of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974 or of a concurrent resolution on the budget. The Budget Process Resolution governs those requirements.","partHeads":[{"heading":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":""}]},{"anchor":"part-2","heading":"SECTION 2. The same threshold governs changes to the rules","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a)** The threshold stated in section 1 applies equally to a measure or motion to amend the Standing Rules of the Senate or a standing order of the Senate.\n\n**(b) A change to the period waits thirty days of session.** A change to section 3, 3A, or 3B(b), to the sentence section 3B(a) inserts in paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, or to the SCHEDULE, made after this resolution is adopted applies only to a measure, group, or nomination on which the motion to proceed is made, or cloture is filed, or, for a conference report, a motion on an amendment of the House, or another measure whose period under section 3(c) begins when the question is first pending, the question is first pending, on or after the thirtieth day on which the Senate is in session after the day the change is adopted or, for a change made by a concurrent resolution, the day on which the two Houses have agreed to the concurrent resolution in the same text, including by agreement to an amendment of the other House or to a conference report. Where a later motion to proceed begins a new period under section 3(b), or cloture is filed again on a nomination or executive resolution, the date of the later motion or filing governs. A resolution or standing order that provides, for a measure, group, or nomination, or for a class of them, a procedure other than the one provided by section 3, 3A, or 3B(b), by the sentence section 3B(a) inserts in paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, or by the SCHEDULE is a change to that provision for the purposes of this subsection; an executive resolution to which section 3B(b) applies is not. This subsection applies to a change to this subsection. It does not apply to the adoption of the Budget Process Resolution or of the Committee Organizing Resolution, or, while section 3(b) of the Budget Process Resolution is in effect, to a provision of a concurrent resolution on the budget; that subsection governs when such a provision applies."},{"anchor":"part-3","heading":"SECTION 3. A fixed period of consideration, ending in a vote","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a) Measures to which this section applies.** This section applies to a bill, a joint resolution, a concurrent resolution, and a resolution, and to a conference report on, or a motion to concur in or to disagree to an amendment of the House of Representatives to, any of them. Each is a measure.\n\n**(b) The motion to proceed.** A motion to proceed to the consideration of a measure is decided without debate. If the motion is not agreed to, or is withdrawn, the period ends without a vote under subsection (f), and a later motion to proceed to the measure begins a new period. A measure that returns to the Calendar without being disposed of, on objection on the call of the Calendar under paragraph 1 of Rule VIII, when that call ends, or by order of the Senate, is not laid aside under subsection (k); its period ends without a vote under subsection (f), and a later motion to proceed to the measure begins a new period.\n\n**(c) The period.** Consideration of a measure lasts for the period of consideration stated in the SCHEDULE, which begins when the motion to proceed is made, or, for a conference report, a motion on an amendment of the House, or any other measure before the Senate without a motion to proceed, other than a resolution or concurrent resolution that came over, under the rule, from an earlier legislative day, when the question is first pending. The period runs only while the Senate is in legislative session with the measure, or the motion to proceed to it, before the Senate and not laid aside under subsection (k), and it runs whether or not any Senator seeks recognition; and it counts every proceeding while the measure is before the Senate, including debate, quorum calls, votes, motions, points of order, and appeals. Time in executive session or on other business does not count, although the measure remains the pending or unfinished business.\n\n**(d) The two halves.** The time is divided equally between the majority leader and the minority leader, or their designees.\n\n(1) Time consumed by a quorum call, a motion, a point of order, or an appeal is charged to the side of the Senator who suggested or made it, and time consumed in reading an amendment is charged to the side of its mover, except that time consumed in debate on any of them is charged to the side that yielded the time to the Senator debating, or, if no time was yielded, as paragraph (4) provides.\n\n(2) Time consumed by a vote is charged to the side of the Senator who offered the question voted on, except that a vote on a guaranteed amendment is charged equally to both sides, and time for which a vote is held open beyond 15 minutes is charged to the majority.\n\n(3) The majority leader or the majority leader's designee may yield back any part of the majority's time. Only the minority leader may yield back any part of the minority's time. The two leaders, acting together, may yield back all remaining time.\n\n(4) Time consumed in debate by a Senator to whom no time has been yielded is charged to that Senator's side. Time not otherwise charged, including time in which no Senator seeks recognition, is charged equally to both sides, and wholly to one side once the other side's time has been used or yielded back.\n\n(5) After a side's time has been used or yielded back, and while the measure, or the motion to proceed to it, is before the Senate, no Senator of that side may debate, suggest the absence of a quorum, make a motion, or appeal from a ruling of the Chair, except that the Senator designated under subsection (e) may appeal from a ruling of the Chair that a guaranteed amendment is not in order, and the majority leader or the majority leader's designee may appeal from a ruling of the Chair and may move to recess, to adjourn, to proceed to executive session, or to lay aside the measure under subsection (k), and, when a quorum is not present, to direct the Sergeant at Arms to request or to compel the attendance of absent Senators. A motion or appeal so excepted is decided without debate, and the time it and any vote on it consume is charged to neither side.\n\n(6) The period ends when the time of both sides has been used or yielded back.\n\n**(e) Guaranteed amendments.** During the period, **the Senator designated by the minority leader is entitled to call up for a vote the number of amendments stated in the SCHEDULE**, each germane to the measure. Debate on each is limited to the time stated in the SCHEDULE, divided equally between the mover and the manager of the measure and charged to their respective sides. **The right is not subject to a motion to table the underlying measure and may not be foreclosed by the filling of the amendment tree.** No amendment to a guaranteed amendment is in order, and a motion to table a guaranteed amendment is not in order until the debate time on it has expired or been yielded back. A guaranteed amendment not called up before the minority's time has been used or yielded back is waived. The Senator designated may submit a guaranteed amendment at the desk while the motion to proceed is pending, naming, for a group, the measure to which it is germane; when the motion is agreed to, each amendment so submitted is called up in the order submitted and is not waived by time used before then. This subsection does not apply to a bill or joint resolution returned by the President with objections, and no amendment to such a bill or joint resolution is in order.\n\n**(f) The vote.** When the period ends, the Senate proceeds, notwithstanding paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, Rule VII, paragraph 1 of Rule VIII, and Rule X, without intervening motion or debate, to vote on each amendment then pending and on any guaranteed amendment called up and not disposed of, and then on the final disposition of the measure and, under paragraph 8 of Rule XIV, on its preamble, if any. Any other question then pending, including a motion, a point of order submitted to the Senate, and an appeal, is put without debate, in the order the precedents provide, and before the vote on the final disposition of the measure to which it relates. Each vote on a guaranteed amendment, and each vote under this subsection, under subsection (g), or under section 3A(d), is taken by the yeas and nays, except a vote taken by ballot under paragraph 2 of Rule XXIV. The time taken by proceedings under this subsection, under subsection (g), or under section 3A(d) does not count toward the Morning Hour. **Notwithstanding Rule VII, Rule VIII, paragraph 1 of Rule XIII, paragraph 7 of Rule XIV, and paragraph 1 of Rule XXII: no motion to postpone the measure, or a question relating to it, is in order during the period, or after it ends and before the final disposition of the measure; no motion to table the measure, a measure in a group under section 3A, or an amendment of the House of Representatives on which the measure is a motion, is in order during the period, or after it ends and before the final disposition of the measure, except a motion by the majority leader or the majority leader's designee made after the minority's time has been used or yielded back and while no guaranteed amendment called up under subsection (e) remains to be disposed of, which is in order whether or not time remains to the majority, notwithstanding subsection (d)(5) and the requirement of this subsection and of subsection (g) that the Senate proceed without intervening motion; no motion to proceed to other business, including a motion to proceed to executive session, a motion under paragraph 3 of Rule VII to lay a matter before the Senate, and a motion to proceed that a statute providing expedited procedure makes in order at any time, is in order while the measure, or the motion to proceed to it, is before the Senate during the period, or during the Morning Hour of a legislative day on which the measure is the unfinished business during its period, except a motion by the majority leader or the majority leader's designee; no motion to proceed to other business, including a motion to proceed to executive session or to lay aside the measure under subsection (k), is in order after the period ends and while a proceeding that this subsection or section 3A(d) requires on the measure remains to be held and is not postponed under subsection (g); no motion to commit or to recommit the measure is in order during the period, or after it ends and before the final disposition of the measure; and no motion to reconsider is in order on the vote on the motion to proceed to the measure, on any other vote on a question relating to the measure taken during the period, or on a vote under this subsection, under subsection (g), or under section 3A(d), except that one motion to reconsider a vote under this subsection, under subsection (g), or under section 3A(d) by which the Senate rejected the final disposition of a measure, or rejected a motion to waive a point of order against a measure, is in order at any time on the calendar day of the vote, notwithstanding subsection (d)(5), if made by the majority leader or the majority leader's designee, whether or not paragraph 1 of Rule XIII would permit that Senator to make it.** The motion is decided without debate, and if it is agreed to, the question reconsidered recurs at once and is decided without debate. If on reconsideration the Senate agrees to a motion to waive a point of order, a ruling sustaining that point of order is vacated, and the proceedings that this subsection, subsection (g), or section 3A(d) requires on the measure and not yet held are held at once. A motion to reconsider that this subsection permits on the calendar day of the vote may be made, and is decided, while another measure or group is before the Senate during its period; that measure or group is then laid aside under subsection (k), and the Senate resumes it in the same way when the motion, and any proceedings it requires, are completed. **Notwithstanding paragraph 3 of Rule XV, no question on a measure or group considered under this section or section 3A, including a question on an amendment or a motion, and no question under section 3B(b), is divisible**, except as section 3A(d) provides for a separate vote on each measure in a group. No dilatory motion, and no dilatory point of order, relating to the measure is in order during the period or after it ends. A point of order that the Chair submits to the Senate is decided without debate once the time of the side of the Senator who raised it has been used or yielded back, or the period has ended. A point of order that the Chair would submit to the Senate, other than a point of order that the Constitution requires more than a majority of those voting to agree to the amendment or to pass, adopt, or agree to the measure, if made by a Senator after the time of that Senator's side has been used or yielded back, or made after the period ends, is not submitted separately: the vote on the amendment, or on the final disposition of the measure, against which it is made decides it, and agreement to the amendment, or passage or adoption of, or agreement to, the measure, decides that the point of order is not well taken; a decision under this sentence is not a precedent of the Senate on the question of order raised. A point of order awaiting that vote does not bar the making of another point of order. Any other point of order pending before the Chair when the period ends, or made after it ends, is decided forthwith. Each measure passed is messaged to the House of Representatives forthwith.\n\n**(g) Postponement to a voting time.** Notwithstanding subsection (f), the majority leader or the majority leader's designee may postpone to a time certain not later than 24 hours after the period ends all of the proceedings that subsection (f) requires when the period ends, including each vote on an amendment, on any other question then pending, and on the final disposition of the measure. The postponement is made by announcement to the Senate at any time during the period, or after it ends and before the first of those proceedings begins; the announcement is not a motion, is not debatable, and is not barred by subsection (d)(5). A later announcement for the same proceedings supersedes an earlier one. Those proceedings may not be held before the period ends; if the period has not ended at the time fixed, the time fixed is the next occurrence of that hour after the period ends, and a time fixed later than 24 hours after the period ends is 24 hours after the period ends. After the period ends, and while they are postponed, the measure is laid aside and the Senate may conduct other business; a postponement announced during the period takes effect when the period ends. At the time fixed, or when the Senate next sits if it is not then in session, or, if the Senate is then sitting for the trial of an impeachment, when that sitting adjourns, the Senate, returning to legislative session for the purpose if it is in executive session, proceeds to them, notwithstanding paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, Rule VII, paragraph 1 of Rule VIII, and Rule X, in the order subsection (f) provides, without intervening motion or debate. Any business then pending is suspended, a measure or group being laid aside under subsection (k), and resumes when they are completed, except that if the business then pending is a measure that subsection (j) excludes as a measure considered under a statute providing expedited procedure, those proceedings are held when that measure is disposed of or, without being disposed of, ceases to be the pending or unfinished business. Postponed proceedings that fall due while other proceedings under subsection (f), this subsection, or section 3A(d) are being held, or while any other vote is being taken, are held after those proceedings or that vote. Postponed proceedings to be held at the same time are held in the order of the times fixed for them, and those with the same time fixed are held in the order in which they were postponed.\n\n**(h) Reading, and amendments to a bill, joint resolution, or group.** Each reading of a measure required by Rule XIV, other than the third reading, is deemed to have occurred when the motion to proceed is agreed to; the engrossment of the measure, or of the amendments to it, where engrossment is required, and its third reading are deemed ordered, and the third reading is deemed to have occurred, immediately before the vote on the final disposition of the measure. During the period of consideration of a measure that is a bill or a joint resolution, or of a group under section 3A, and until the votes on it under subsection (f), subsection (g), or section 3A(d) are completed, no amendment is in order other than a guaranteed amendment under subsection (e). An amendment reported by a committee to such a measure, including an amendment in the nature of a substitute, is deemed agreed to when the motion to proceed is agreed to, and the measure as so amended is treated as original text for the purpose of a guaranteed amendment under subsection (e). The reading of an amendment, including an amendment of the House, is dispensed with if it has been available in printed form at the desk for not less than 24 hours. For a measure whose period under subsection (c) begins when the question is first pending, a reading, or an amendment reported by a committee, that this subsection deems to have occurred, or to be agreed to, when the motion to proceed is agreed to is deemed to have occurred, or to be agreed to, when the question is first pending.\n\n**(i) Cloture and waiver.** Notwithstanding paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, a cloture motion on a measure governed by this section is not in order. **No motion to waive or to suspend, as to a single measure, group, or nomination, any provision of this section, of section 3A, of section 3B(b), or of the sentence section 3B(a) inserts in paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, is in order.** The Senate may change those provisions only by resolution.\n\n**(j) What this section does not reach.** This section does not apply to a measure considered under a statute providing expedited procedure if\u2014\n\n(1) the statute, as in effect on the date this resolution is adopted, would provide expedited procedure for the measure without regard to any law enacted on or after that date; or\n\n(2) the measure is a joint resolution the matter after the resolving clause of which only disapproves a rule or other action of the executive branch, with or without providing that it shall have no force or effect, or a joint or concurrent resolution the matter after the resolving clause of which only directs the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities;\n\nand section 4 governs those. No amendment to a measure described in paragraph (2) is in order if, were it agreed to, the measure would no longer be so described. Nor does it apply to a concurrent resolution on the budget, to a reconciliation bill or resolution reported under a reconciliation instruction, or to a rescission bill under section 1017 of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974, which the Budget Process Resolution governs, or to an executive resolution, which section 3B governs. Nor does it apply to a concurrent resolution providing for an adjournment of either or both Houses, to an order, resolution, or motion offered while the Senate is sitting for the trial of an impeachment, or to any other measure or matter that is not debatable under the rules and precedents of the Senate in effect on the date this resolution is adopted.\n\n**(k) Laying aside.** The majority leader or the majority leader's designee may move to lay aside a pending measure or group and, in the same motion, to proceed to another measure or to resume a measure or group laid aside under this subsection; the motion names that measure or group, is decided without debate, and is not divisible. When the measure or group so named is disposed of, or its votes are postponed under subsection (g), the Senate resumes consideration of the measure or group the motion laid aside, without intervening motion. A measure or group that ceases to be before the Senate during its period because the Senate proceeds to another measure, other than on a motion under this subsection, whether or not under the precedents it is displaced, is laid aside under this subsection, and the Senate resumes it in the same way when the measure proceeded to is disposed of or its votes are postponed under subsection (g). A measure or group proceeded to during the Morning Hour that is pending during its period when the Morning Hour expires, and that gives way under the precedents to the unfinished business or other business then laid before the Senate, is laid aside under this subsection, and the Senate resumes it in the same way when that business is disposed of or its votes are postponed under subsection (g). If two or more measures or groups would resume at the same time, the Senate resumes the one most recently laid aside, and the others in reverse order of laying aside as each is disposed of or its votes are postponed under subsection (g). A demand for the regular order does not return a measure or group laid aside under this subsection. When the Senate resumes consideration, the period continues with the time then remaining to each side, and a guaranteed amendment not yet called up remains available. Laying aside does not end the period under subsection (b).\n\n**(l) Designees.** For the purposes of this section, section 3A, and section 3B(b), the majority leader and the minority leader may each designate one or more Senators as that leader's designee, and the minority leader may designate the Senator under subsection (e), for a proceeding or generally, by announcement to the Senate or by a writing filed with the Secretary of the Senate. A designation continues until it is revoked in the same way by the leader who made it or by a successor, and a designation filed with the Secretary of the Senate is conclusive. While no designation of a leader's designee applies to a proceeding, a Senator acting as that leader in the leader's absence is that leader's designee for it, and while no designation under subsection (e) applies to a proceeding, the minority leader, or a Senator acting as the minority leader in the minority leader's absence, is the Senator under subsection (e) for it. Where more than one designation under subsection (e) applies to a proceeding, the one made for that proceeding governs over a general one, and a later one governs over an earlier one of the same kind."},{"anchor":"part-3-2","heading":"SECTION 3A. Measures passed by the House, considered in a group","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a) The group.** The majority leader or the majority leader's designee may move to proceed to the consideration, together, of a group of measures, each of which has passed the House of Representatives and is on the calendar, not exceeding the number stated in the SCHEDULE. The motion names each measure and is not divisible.\n\n**(b) One period.** Section 3 applies to the group as if it were one measure, except as this section provides.\n\n**(c) Guaranteed amendments.** The number of amendments stated in the SCHEDULE under section 3(e) is guaranteed for the group. Each must be germane to one measure in the group, and the Senator calling it up names that measure.\n\n**(d) A separate vote on each measure.** When the period ends, or at the time fixed under section 3(g), the Senate votes on each measure separately, in the order named in the motion, after disposing of any amendment to it, and then, under paragraph 8 of Rule XIV, on its preamble, if any. **The vote on one measure does not dispose of any other.** Each measure passed is a separate act of the Senate.\n\n**(e) A point of order against one measure.** A point of order sustained against one measure in the group removes that measure only. The others remain under consideration within the same period.\n\n**(f) Receipt and the calendar.** On the request of the majority leader or the majority leader's designee, a measure passed by the House of Representatives and received in the Senate is read the first and second times and placed on the calendar without further action, and each reading that Rule XIV requires before a measure is placed on the calendar is deemed to have occurred. Notwithstanding paragraph 3 of Rule XIV, a measure so placed may be named in a motion under subsection (a) on any calendar day after the calendar day on which it is placed, whether or not a new legislative day has begun. Notwithstanding paragraph 2 of Rule XIV, a reading deemed to have occurred under this subsection or under section 3(h) satisfies that paragraph, and no demand that the readings of a measure to which this subsection or section 3(h) applies occur on different legislative days is in order."},{"anchor":"part-3-3","heading":"SECTION 3B. Post-cloture time on nominations","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a) Level I nominations.** Paragraph 2 of Rule XXII is amended by inserting after the sentence beginning \"However, only one motion to extend time\" the following:\n\n> **Notwithstanding the thirty hours provided above, after cloture is invoked on a nomination to a position at level I of the Executive Schedule under section 5312 of title 5, United States Code, post-cloture consideration shall not exceed the time stated for that purpose in the schedule of the Majority Rule Resolution, equally divided between and controlled by the Majority and Minority Leaders or their designees.**\n\n**(b) Nominations considered together.** After cloture is invoked on an executive resolution providing for the consideration en bloc of nominations, none of which is to a position at level I of the Executive Schedule, to a court established under Article III of the Constitution, to the office of an inspector general, or to an office the holder of which serves a term fixed by statute and either is, by the terms of a statute, removable only for cause or serves a term of more than four years, post-cloture consideration of the resolution and of the nominations it lists, together, may not exceed the time stated in the SCHEDULE, equally divided. On adoption of the resolution, the Senate proceeds without intervening motion or debate to the question of confirming en bloc the listed nominations then pending, other than a nomination returned under this subsection or named under the next sentence. At any time after cloture is invoked on the resolution and before that question is put, the minority leader, or the minority leader's designee, may name, by announcement to the Senate or by a writing filed with the Secretary of the Senate, not more than the number of the listed nominations stated in the SCHEDULE; after the question of confirming en bloc is decided, the Senate proceeds without intervening motion or debate to the question of confirming each nomination so named, separately, in the order named. Where more nominations are named than the number stated in the SCHEDULE, those first named, up to that number, are the nominations named, and the order named is the order in which they were named. Before putting the question of confirming en bloc, the Presiding Officer states the nominations named, including each named by a writing filed with the Secretary of the Senate before that statement. A point of order against one listed nomination, sustained before the question on it is put, returns that nomination to the Executive Calendar or to committee, as the point of order requires, and the Senate proceeds on the others."},{"anchor":"part-4","heading":"SECTION 4. What is untouched","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a) Thresholds and procedure are distinguished, and section 1(b) reaches only the first.** Except as sections 3 and 3B provide, nothing in this resolution alters the privileged status of a measure, the time limits of an expedited procedure that section 3(j) excludes, a requirement of germaneness, or the disposition of a nomination. **A statute providing expedited procedure continues to operate according to its terms, except that a requirement within it of more than a majority of those voting is of no effect in the Senate**, subject to section 1(d), and except that a motion to proceed it makes in order at any time is in order only as section 3(f) provides.\n\n**(b) Constitutional thresholds are beyond the reach of any rule.** **A requirement of the Constitution that a question be decided by more than a majority of those voting is fixed by the Constitution and is untouched by this resolution, including the requirement of two-thirds for conviction on impeachment, for the Senate's advice and consent to a treaty, for the expulsion of a Member, for the proposal of an amendment to the Constitution, for passage over the objection of the President, for the removal of a disability under section 3 of the Fourteenth Amendment, and for a decision under section 4 of the Twenty-Fifth Amendment, and the quorum and majority of the whole number of Senators required to choose a Vice President under the Twelfth Amendment.** No rule of the Senate can alter them, and this resolution does not purport to.\n\n**(c) The distinction.** **A supermajority written by the Senate is displaced. A supermajority written by the Constitution is not.** Where a threshold is said to arise from a statute, the question is whether the statute was enacted as an exercise of the rulemaking power \u2014 **if it was, it is a rule of the Senate whatever it is called, and section 1(b) governs it**, subject to section 1(d)."},{"anchor":"part-4-2","heading":"SECTION 4A. Committee meetings while the Senate sits","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"Paragraph 5(a) of Rule XXVI is amended to read as follows:\n\n> 5. (a) Notwithstanding any other provision of the rules, when the Senate is in session, no committee of the Senate or any subcommittee thereof may meet, without special leave, after the conclusion of the first two hours after the meeting of the Senate commenced and in no case after two o'clock postmeridian unless consent therefor has been obtained from the majority leader ~~and the minority leader (or in the event of the absence of either of such leaders, from his designee)~~ **(or, in the event of the majority leader's absence, from the majority leader's designee)**. The prohibition contained in the preceding sentence shall not apply to the Committee on Appropriations or the Committee on the Budget. The majority leader or his designee shall announce to the Senate whenever consent has been given under this subparagraph and shall state the time and place of such meeting. The right to make such announcement of consent shall have the same priority as the filing of a cloture motion."},{"anchor":"part-4-3","heading":"SECTION 4B. Standing orders","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"The provisions of this resolution that do not amend the text of a Standing Rule of the Senate, and the SCHEDULE, are standing orders of the Senate. They continue from one Congress to the next until changed as this resolution provides."},{"anchor":"part-5","heading":"SECTION 5. Effective date and short title","part":"A RESOLUTION AMENDING THE STANDING RULES OF THE SENATE","body":"**(a)** This resolution takes effect on the date of its adoption and applies to any measure pending on that date.\n\n**(b)** For a measure pending on that date, the period of consideration under section 3 begins on that date.\n\n**(c)** This resolution may be cited as the Majority Rule Resolution."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"| Ref | Provision | Value | *(Reserved.)* |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| S-1 | \u00a7 3(c) period of consideration of a measure or group | 12 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-2 | \u00a7 3(e) guaranteed minority amendments, per measure or group | 2 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-3 | \u00a7 3(e) debate on each guaranteed amendment | 2 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-4 | \u00a7 3A(a) measures in a group | 6 | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-5 | \u00a7 3B(a) post-cloture time on a level I nomination | 8 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-6 | \u00a7 3B(b) post-cloture time on an en bloc nominations resolution | 2 hours | *(Reserved.)* |\n| S-7 | \u00a7 3B(b) listed nominations the minority leader may name for separate votes, per resolution | 2 | *(Reserved.)* |"},{"anchor":"part-1-2","heading":"SECTION 1. Budget Act thresholds by majority","part":"THE BUDGET PROCESS RESOLUTION","body":"**(a)** Notwithstanding section 1(d) of the Majority Rule Resolution, section 1(b) of that resolution applies to a requirement of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974 or of a concurrent resolution on the budget, including a motion to waive or to suspend a point of order, and an appeal from a ruling of the Chair on a point of order, arising under that Act or under such a resolution. **Section 904 of that Act, as a rule of the Senate, is of no further effect in the Senate to the extent that it requires more than a majority of the Senators voting.**\n\n**(b) The Byrd rule.** Subsection (a) reaches section 313 of that Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"THE BUDGET PROCESS RESOLUTION","body":"*A separate resolution, referred as the standing orders for legislation affecting the budget process require.*"}]},{"anchor":"part-2-2","heading":"SECTION 2. Application of the guarantees to budget procedures","part":"THE BUDGET PROCESS RESOLUTION","body":"**(a)** Section 3(e) of the Majority Rule Resolution applies to a concurrent resolution on the budget, to a reconciliation bill or resolution reported under a reconciliation instruction, and to a rescission bill under section 1017 of the Congressional Budget and Impoundment Control Act of 1974. **Where that Act, such a resolution, or such an instruction provides a shorter period of consideration, or fewer amendments, than the SCHEDULE of that resolution requires, the SCHEDULE governs, except that a provision of a concurrent resolution on the budget agreed to after this resolution is adopted governs as section 3(b) provides.** A longer period that the Act, resolution, or instruction provides is not shortened.\n\n**(b) A point of order arising from an amendment agreed to after the period.** When a point of order under that Act or under a concurrent resolution on the budget is made against a measure after the period of consideration under section 3 of the Majority Rule Resolution has ended, or after the majority's time in that period has been used or yielded back, and the point of order lies only because of an amendment agreed to after the majority's time was used or yielded back, the majority leader or the majority leader's designee may move to waive it, notwithstanding sections 3(d)(5) and 3(f) of that resolution. The motion is decided without debate before the point of order is decided, and the time it and any vote on it consume is charged to neither side. A vote on the motion is a vote under section 3(f) of that resolution. Section 3(l) of that resolution applies to the majority leader's designee under this subsection as it applies under section 3 of that resolution.\n\n**(c) Motions to waive pending together.** During the period of consideration of a measure or group under section 3 or 3A of the Majority Rule Resolution, and notwithstanding paragraph 1 of Rule XXII, a motion to waive a point of order under that Act or under a concurrent resolution on the budget with respect to one measure, or to one amendment, is in order whether or not time for debate remains on the measure or amendment to which it relates, and while another question on the measure or group is pending, including such a motion with respect to a different measure or amendment. The motion remains pending, and the Senate proceeds to other questions on the measure or group, until the period ends; it is then put under section 3(f) of that resolution, before the vote on the measure or amendment to which it relates, and a vote on it is a vote under that subsection. Notwithstanding paragraph 1 of Rule XXII, no motion to table such a motion is in order. While such a motion is pending, a ruling on a point of order that it would waive is stayed until the motion is decided, notwithstanding section 3(f) of that resolution. A point of order whose ruling is stayed under this subsection does not bar the making of another point of order."},{"anchor":"part-3-4","heading":"SECTION 3. Effective date, standing order, and short title","part":"THE BUDGET PROCESS RESOLUTION","body":"**(a)** This resolution takes effect on the date of its adoption and applies to any measure pending on that date.\n\n**(b)** This resolution is a standing order of the Senate. It continues from one Congress to the next until changed. A change to section 2 of this resolution made after this resolution is adopted, and a provision of a concurrent resolution on the budget giving a measure a shorter period of consideration, or fewer amendments, than section 2 allows, applies only to a measure on which the motion to proceed is made, or, for a conference report, a motion on an amendment of the House of Representatives, or another measure whose period under section 3(c) of the Majority Rule Resolution begins when the question is first pending, the question is first pending, on or after the thirtieth day on which the Senate is in session after the day the change is adopted or, for a change made by a concurrent resolution, including a concurrent resolution on the budget, the day on which the two Houses have agreed to the concurrent resolution in the same text, including by agreement to an amendment of the other House or to a conference report. A resolution or standing order that provides, for a measure or a class of measures, a procedure other than the one section 2 provides is a change to section 2 for the purposes of this subsection. A provision of a concurrent resolution on the budget that changes section 2(b), 3, 3A, or 3B(b) of the Majority Rule Resolution, the sentence section 3B(a) of that resolution inserts in paragraph 2 of Rule XXII, or the SCHEDULE of that resolution, or that provides, for a measure, group, or nomination, or for a class of them, a procedure other than the one provided by any of them, applies only to a measure, group, or nomination on which the motion to proceed is made, or cloture is filed, or, for a conference report, a motion on an amendment of the House of Representatives, or another measure whose period under section 3(c) of the Majority Rule Resolution begins when the question is first pending, the question is first pending, on or after the thirtieth day on which the Senate is in session after the day on which the two Houses have agreed to the concurrent resolution in the same text, including by agreement to an amendment of the other House or to a conference report. Where a later motion to proceed to a measure or group is made after an earlier one is not agreed to or is withdrawn, or after the measure returns to the Calendar as section 3(b) of the Majority Rule Resolution provides, or cloture is filed again on a nomination or executive resolution, the date of the later motion or filing governs. This subsection applies to a change to this subsection.\n\n**(c)** This resolution may be cited as the Budget Process Resolution."},{"anchor":"part-1-3","heading":"SECTION 1. Committee membership","part":"THE COMMITTEE ORGANIZING RESOLUTION","body":"**(a) Appointment.** Notwithstanding paragraphs 2, 3, and 4 of Rule XXV, for the Congress in which this resolution is adopted, the Senators named in the ANNEX are appointed members of the standing committees named there, and the Senator so designated is appointed chair of each.\n\n**(b) The minority's seats.** A Senator designated by the minority leader for a seat the ANNEX assigns to the minority is appointed to that seat when the minority leader files the designation with the Secretary of the Senate, without further action by the Senate.\n\n**(c) A minority seat not filled.** While a seat the ANNEX assigns to the minority on a committee is not filled\u2014\n\n(1) the seat is not counted in the entire membership of the committee, or in the membership of the committee, for the purposes of paragraph 7(a) of Rule XXVI or of a rule of the committee fixing a quorum or the number of members who must be present to report a measure or matter; and\n\n(2) a rule of the committee, or of a subcommittee of it, requiring that a number of members of the minority be present, or vote, for the committee or subcommittee to meet, transact business, take testimony, or report a measure or matter is satisfied by the presence or vote of the lesser of that number and the number of members of the minority then serving on the committee or subcommittee.\n\n**(d) Minority members absent.** At a meeting of a committee or subcommittee of which notice was given as the rules of the Senate and of the committee require, and not less than three calendar days before the meeting, a rule described in subsection (c)(2) is satisfied by the presence or vote of the members of the minority who attend, or, if none attends, without the presence or vote of any member of the minority.\n\n**(e) Closing debate in committee.** During the Congress in which this resolution is adopted, at a meeting described in subsection (d), once a measure, matter, or nomination has been debated for two hours in all at that meeting and at any earlier meeting described in subsection (d) of the committee or subcommittee holding that meeting, in the same Congress, counting only time in debate on it or on an amendment to it, and not time taking testimony, as shown by the transcript or recording of each such meeting, kept by the committee or subcommittee holding it in the form paragraph 5(e)(1) of Rule XXVI requires of a committee, a motion to bring it, and any amendment then pending to it, to a vote without further debate is in order, is decided without debate, and is agreed to by a majority of the members voting, a quorum for the transaction of business being present, notwithstanding any rule of the committee or subcommittee requiring that debate be closed by a greater number of votes or by the vote of a member of the minority. The motion is not in order while a member of the minority who is present, and who has sought recognition at that meeting or at an earlier meeting at which time is counted under this subsection, by a request stated on the record or delivered in writing to the clerk of the committee or subcommittee, to debate the measure, matter, or nomination, or an amendment to it, has not been recognized since seeking it, unless members of the minority have been recognized for debate on it, or on an amendment to it, for one hour in all of the time counted under this subsection. The clerk of the committee or subcommittee keeps the time counted under this subsection, and the part of it in which members of the minority were recognized, and states each on the request of any member. This subsection confers no right on any person other than a Senator.\n\n**(f) A chair not appointed.** If the Senate, on a separate vote under paragraph 1 of Rule XXIV, rejects the appointment of the Senator the ANNEX designates as chair of a committee, or agrees to an amendment that strikes that designation and designates no other Senator as chair, the first Senator listed among that committee's majority members in the ANNEX who is a member of the committee, other than the Senator rejected or struck, serves as chair of the committee, notwithstanding paragraph 4(e)(1) of Rule XXV, for all purposes of the rules of the Senate and of the committee and of any law, until the Senate appoints a chair, and the Senator rejected or struck does not serve as chair of the committee in that Congress by continuation from the preceding Congress.","partHeads":[{"heading":"THE COMMITTEE ORGANIZING RESOLUTION","body":"*A separate resolution, for the Congress in which it is adopted.*"}]},{"anchor":"part-2-3","heading":"SECTION 2. Signing enrolled measures, receiving messages, and nominations","part":"THE COMMITTEE ORGANIZING RESOLUTION","body":"During the Congress in which this resolution is adopted, the Vice President, the President pro tempore, and the Acting President pro tempore are each authorized to sign duly enrolled bills and joint resolutions while the Senate is in recess or adjournment. During that Congress, while the Senate is in recess or adjournment, the Secretary of the Senate is authorized to receive messages from the President of the United States and, except for House bills, joint resolutions, and concurrent resolutions, messages from the House of Representatives, and they shall be appropriately referred. During that Congress, a nomination received from the President may be referred to the appropriate committee on the day it is received, whether or not the Senate is in executive session, and a committee's report on a nomination may be filed with the Secretary of the Senate, and the nomination placed on the Executive Calendar, on any day, whether or not the Senate is in session or in executive session. During that Congress, notwithstanding paragraph 4 of Rule XXXI, the Secretary of the Senate shall notify the President forthwith of each nomination confirmed or rejected by the Senate."},{"anchor":"part-3-5","heading":"SECTION 3. Effective date and short title","part":"THE COMMITTEE ORGANIZING RESOLUTION","body":"This resolution takes effect on the date of its adoption. This resolution may be cited as the Committee Organizing Resolution."},{"anchor":"annex","heading":"ANNEX","part":"THE COMMITTEE ORGANIZING RESOLUTION","body":"| Committee | Chair | Majority members, in the order section 1(f) applies | Minority seats |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| *(Reserved: filled from the majority conference's assignments before introduction.)* | | | |"},{"anchor":"part-1-4","heading":"SECTION 1. Orders of business for the first bills of the Congress","part":"THE HOUSE ORDERS FOR THE FIRST BILLS","body":"**(a) The rules resolution.** Upon adoption of this resolution it shall be in order to consider in the House the resolution (H. Res. __) adopting the Rules of the House of Representatives for the One Hundred Twenty-First Congress. The resolution shall be considered as read.\n\n**(b) The first bills.** Upon adoption of this resolution it shall be in order to consider in the House any bill specified in subsection (c). All points of order against consideration of each such bill are waived. Each such bill shall be considered as read. All points of order against provisions in each such bill are waived. The previous question shall be considered as ordered on each such bill to final passage without intervening motion except: (1) one hour of debate equally divided and controlled by the Majority Leader and the Minority Leader or their respective designees; and (2) one motion to recommit.\n\n**(c) The list.** The bills referred to in subsection (b) are as follows:\n\n(1) The bill (H.R. __), the Personal Privacy Act.\n\n(2) The bill (H.R. __), the Data Warrant Act.\n\n(3) The bill (H.R. __), the AI Safety Act.\n\n(4) The bill (H.R. __), the Pentagon Accountability Act.\n\n(5) The bill (H.R. __), the Cyber Defense Act.\n\n(6) The bill (H.R. __), the American Influence Act.\n\n(7) The bill (H.R. __), the Human Decision in Warfare Act.\n\n(8) The bill (H.R. __), the Executive Integrity Act.\n\n(9) The bill (H.R. __), the Congressional Integrity Act.\n\n(10) The bill (H.R. __), the Judiciary Integrity Act.\n\n(11) The bill (H.R. __), the Candidate Integrity Act.\n\n(12) The bill (H.R. __), the Pardon Transparency Act.\n\n(13) The bill (H.R. __), the Enforceable Rights Act.\n\n(14) The bill (H.R. __), the Accountable Government Act.\n\n(15) The bill (H.R. __), the Confirmed Officials Act.\n\n(16) The bill (H.R. __), the Independent Investigators Act.\n\n(17) The bill (H.R. __), the Emergency and War Powers Act.\n\n(18) The bill (H.R. __), the Fair Representation Act.\n\n(19) The bill (H.R. __), the People's House Act.\n\n(20) The bill (H.R. __), the John R. Lewis Voting Rights Act.\n\n(21) The bill (H.R. __), the Count the Votes Act.\n\n(22) The bill (H.R. __), the Free Photo ID for Voting and Everyday Life Act.","partHeads":[{"heading":"THE HOUSE ORDERS FOR THE FIRST BILLS","body":"*Section 1 is a separate resolution, adopted on the opening day before the resolution adopting the Rules of the House of Representatives for the One Hundred Twenty-First Congress. Sections 2 and 3 are offered in that rules resolution, each numbered in its sequence.*"}]},{"anchor":"part-2-4","heading":"SECTION 2. Separate order: hearings held in the prior Congress","part":"THE HOUSE ORDERS FOR THE FIRST BILLS","body":"During the One Hundred Twenty-First Congress, a hearing that may be listed and designated under clause 3(c)(6)(A) of rule XIII for a bill or joint resolution includes a hearing held during the One Hundred Twentieth Congress by a committee or subcommittee of the House or of the Senate on a measure the text of which is the same as, or substantially the same as, the text of that bill or joint resolution. This section does not limit any hearing that may otherwise be listed or designated under that clause."},{"anchor":"part-3-6","heading":"SECTION 3. Passage of income tax rate increases by majority","part":"THE HOUSE ORDERS FOR THE FIRST BILLS","body":"Clause 5 of rule XXI is amended by striking the paragraph, if any, that requires a vote of more than a majority of the Members voting for a bill, joint resolution, amendment, or conference report carrying a Federal income tax rate increase to be considered as passed or agreed to, and by redesignating any later paragraph of that clause accordingly."}],"asOf":"2026-11-18"}}}};
